---
// The toy: reads the live forecast for Linda Mar, makes a call, and shows the reading it made the
// call from.
//
// Structure note. An earlier version rendered four hand-drawn states and revealed one, which meant
// the picture was identical on a two-foot day and a fifteen-foot day and the actual forecast
// numbers were computed and then thrown away. This version renders one layout and draws it from
// the data, so the shape of the swell on screen is the shape of the swell in the forecast.
//
// The only live third-party fetch on this site. Everything else reads JSON frozen at collection
// time; a "should I surf right now" widget showing conditions from the last deploy would be worse
// than useless, so this one exception is called out in the Methodology.
import playlistData from "../../../../public/data/surf-forecast/playlists.json";
import { LINDA_MAR } from "../../../lib/surf-forecast/spots";

const playlists = playlistData.playlists;
---

<section id="linda-mar" class="mx-auto max-w-7xl px-6 py-16 md:px-12 md:py-20">
	<h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight md:text-3xl">Should I go surf right now?</h2>
	<div class="mt-3 max-w-[65ch] space-y-4">
		<p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-ink-soft">
			My home break is Linda Mar, in Pacifica. It is an exposed beach break that is small most of the time and
			blown out a good deal of the rest, so the honest answer to "should I paddle out" is usually no. This reads
			the live forecast for the beach, makes the call, and then shows the reading it made the call from, because a
			verdict you cannot check is just an opinion.
		</p>
		<p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-ink-soft">
			The strip at the bottom is the forecast drawn to scale, not decoration. Crest spacing is the real
			deep-water wavelength, so a short-period windswell draws packed chatter and a long-period groundswell draws
			one slow wall. The crests advance at the actual wave period. Onshore wind roughens the surface and offshore
			wind blows spray back off the top, which is the detail that matters most here: at this beach the wind
			decides more than the size does.
		</p>
	</div>

	<div id="toy-root" class="mt-8 border border-line" data-tier="loading">
		<div class="flex flex-wrap items-baseline justify-between gap-x-6 gap-y-1 border-b border-line px-6 py-4">
			<p class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-wide" data-toy-eyebrow>Reading the forecast</p>
			<p class="font-mono text-xs text-ink-soft" data-toy-stamp>{LINDA_MAR.name}</p>
		</div>

		<div class="md:grid md:grid-cols-[1fr_19rem]">
			<div class="p-6 md:p-8">
				<p class="font-display text-2xl font-semibold md:text-3xl" data-toy-headline>Checking the water</p>
				<ul class="mt-4 space-y-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-soft" data-toy-reasons>
					<li class="h-4 w-3/4 animate-pulse bg-line/40"></li>
					<li class="h-4 w-1/2 animate-pulse bg-line/30"></li>
				</ul>
				<p class="mt-6 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink" data-toy-joke></p>
			</div>

			<div class="border-t border-line p-6 md:border-l md:border-t-0" data-toy-readout>
				<dl class="grid grid-cols-2 gap-x-4 gap-y-3 md:grid-cols-1" data-toy-numbers></dl>
				<div class="mt-5" data-toy-compass></div>
			</div>
		</div>

		<figure class="border-t border-line">
			<div class="bg-line/10" data-toy-diagram></div>
			<figcaption class="px-6 py-3 font-mono text-[0.7rem] leading-relaxed text-ink-soft" data-toy-caption>
				One frame spans about 400 m of open water.
			</figcaption>
		</figure>

		{
			playlists.map((p) => (
				<div class="hidden border-t border-line p-6" data-toy-playlist={p.tier}>
					<p class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-wide text-ink-soft">Soundtrack</p>
					<div class="mt-2 flex flex-wrap items-baseline justify-between gap-x-6 gap-y-2">
						<div class="max-w-[48ch]">
							<p class="font-display text-lg font-semibold">{p.name}</p>
							<p class="mt-1 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-soft">{p.rationale}</p>
						</div>
						<button
							type="button"
							class="shrink-0 border border-line px-4 py-2 font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-wide transition-colors hover:border-ink hover:text-ink"
							data-toy-load-player={p.embed_url}
							data-playlist-name={p.name}
						>
							Load player
						</button>
					</div>
					<p class="mt-3 font-mono text-[0.7rem] text-ink-soft">
						Loads from Spotify only when you press the button, so the page makes no third-party requests
						until you ask it to.
					</p>
					<div data-toy-player-slot />
				</div>
			))
		}
	</div>

	<noscript>
		<p class="mt-4 max-w-[65ch] text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-soft">
			This one needs JavaScript, because it reads the forecast when you open the page rather than when the site
			was built. The
			<a
				href="https://www.ndbc.noaa.gov/station_page.php?station=46237"
				class="text-accent-blue underline underline-offset-2">San Francisco Bar buoy</a
			> is the nearest real measurement.
		</p>
	</noscript>

	<p class="mt-4 max-w-[65ch] font-mono text-xs leading-relaxed text-ink-soft">
		Live from Open-Meteo's marine and weather models, sampled at {LINDA_MAR.latitude}, {LINDA_MAR.longitude} and
		re-read each hour. This is a model forecast rather than a measurement: the nearest wave buoy is the San
		Francisco Bar station, 24 km north, which is why Linda Mar sits in the marginal tier in the table below rather
		than the close one.
	</p>
</section>

<style>
	/* Scoped rules, for elements that exist in this template and therefore carry Astro's scope
	   attribute. Anything the script creates at runtime is styled in the global block below. */
	#toy-root {
		--toy-accent: var(--color-ink-soft);
	}
	#toy-root[data-tier="junk"] {
		--toy-accent: var(--color-accent-yellow);
	}
	#toy-root[data-tier="fun"] {
		--toy-accent: var(--color-accent-green);
	}
	#toy-root[data-tier="firing"] {
		--toy-accent: var(--color-accent-blue);
	}

	[data-toy-eyebrow] {
		color: var(--toy-accent);
	}

	[data-toy-diagram] {
		line-height: 0;
	}
</style>

<style is:global>
	/* The diagram, compass and Spotify frame are built by the script after hydration, so they never
	   receive Astro's scope attribute and scoped selectors silently fail to match them. Found the
	   hard way: the drift animation resolved to `none` and the waves sat frozen while every other
	   style looked fine. Every selector here is namespaced under #toy-root, which is unique to this
	   page, so going global costs nothing in isolation. */
	#toy-root [data-toy-diagram] svg {
		display: block;
		width: 100%;
		height: auto;
	}

	/* Each layer advances exactly one crest spacing per wave period, which is what a real crest
	   does, so the motion carries the same information the spacing does. */
	@keyframes toy-drift {
		from {
			transform: translateX(0);
		}
		to {
			transform: translateX(var(--drift-px, 120px));
		}
	}

	#toy-root .toy-drift {
		animation: toy-drift var(--drift-duration, 12s) linear infinite;
		will-change: transform;
	}

	/* Colour drift only, and only when it is worth going. The geometry never changes with it, so
	   the reading stays truthful while the mood does not. */
	@keyframes toy-hue {
		from {
			filter: hue-rotate(0deg) saturate(1);
		}
		50% {
			filter: hue-rotate(28deg) saturate(1.25);
		}
		to {
			filter: hue-rotate(0deg) saturate(1);
		}
	}

	#toy-root[data-tier="fun"] [data-toy-diagram] svg {
		animation: toy-hue 32s ease-in-out infinite;
	}
	#toy-root[data-tier="firing"] [data-toy-diagram] svg {
		animation: toy-hue 14s ease-in-out infinite;
	}

	@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
		#toy-root .toy-drift,
		#toy-root [data-toy-diagram] svg {
			animation: none !important;
		}
	}

	#toy-root [data-toy-player-slot] iframe {
		margin-top: 1rem;
		width: 100%;
		border: 0;
		border-radius: 0;
	}
</style>

<script src="../../../scripts/surf-forecast/live-conditions-toy.ts"></script>
